64:  We present \textbf{CAM-Sim}, a reproducible paired-seed benchmark for situational-awareness ablations in marketing agents: 50 seeds $\times$ 200 scenarios, eleven agents on a ladder from context-blind baseline through signal-based classifiers to a labeled oracle. Multi-signal situation classification raises match from 87.5\% to 98.7\% and profit from +\$155 to +\$205 (+\$50, \pv{8.5\!\times\!10^{-32}}, $d_z\!=\!3.96$). A matched-family ablation isolates the gain as signal-driven (+\$103.85, \pv{5.16\!\times\!10^{-46}}; +\$103.10 learned-vs-learned, \pv{1.60\!\times\!10^{-45}}). Standard baselines confirm the advantage across three classifier families (LR +\$65.48, \pvl{10^{-40}}; RF +\$75.98, \pvl{10^{-37}}). A context-blind policy search reveals a channel-cost arbitrage: EDUCATIONAL on EMAIL at bid 0.3 earns +\$252, beating every deployable context-aware agent in 7 environments. The advantage replicates across 9 environments (per-seed \srho $\ge0.93$), survives 30\% label noise and budget pacing, checked by paired-difference bootstrap (B $=\!2000$), macro-F1, and common random numbers. v1.5 executes the pre-registered zero-shot LLM baseline (74--83\% accuracy, macro-recall 50--61\% vs. 65.9\%, 4-of-6 blind spots). v1.6 shows blindness is prior-driven, prompt remediation is a trade-off, and end-to-end LLM action selection exceeds \Ag{am_multisignal} with no fitted component (+\$250--273 vs. +\$206--220).
