\textbf{Common random numbers} seed a single global stream per seed, so agent $k$ sees the stream after agents $1\ldots k{-}1$; the context is paired but the decision noise is not. Per-agent RNG (same contexts) changes profits by $<$ \$3 for all stochastic agents (baseline +\$0.81; noisy50 -\$2.62; noisy80 +\$0.86); all deterministic agents are unaffected. Conclusion: paired t-test remains valid.

\subsection{Channel-Dependent Reward Condition}
\label{sec:cdreward}
The channel-cost arbitrage arises because base rewards are indexed by (action, situation), not channel. Adding a channel-mismatch penalty (0.5) drops the arbitrage policy \Ag{b_edu_email_030} from +\$252.41 to +\$132.72 (-47.5\%) while \Ag{am_multisignal} is nearly unchanged (+\$206.13 $\rightarrow$ +\$205.77). The arbitrage disappears: \Ag{am_multisignal} beats it by +\$73.05. This confirms the arbitrage is a reward-design artifact, not a feature of context-aware decisioning.

\subsection{Adversarial-Mapping Condition}
\label{sec:advmap}
An agent with perfect 3-signal classification (98.9\% match) but a deranged action mapping (EXPLORATION$\rightarrow$PROMOTIONAL, \ldots, RETENTION$\rightarrow$URGENT) achieves 0.4\% match against the true ideal action and profit -\$268.88 (worse than the random baseline, -\$170.03). Contrast vs \Ag{am_multisignal}: +\$475.01 (\pv{5.23\!\times\!10^{-96}}, d = 29.28). Perfect classification with a wrong mapping is catastrophic; the benchmark is falsifiable via profit.

\subsection{Contextual-Bandit Baselines}
\label{sec:bandits}
Two offline-trained bandits: (a) $\varepsilon$-greedy (3-signal NCC classification, $\varepsilon{=}0.1$ random action, else Q-argmax) earns +\$169.91 (90.1\% match; -\$36.22 vs \Ag{am_multisignal}, \pv{2.95\!\times\!10^{-31}}); (b) LinUCB-style linear profit model (ridge per action type) earns +\$91.42 (75.6\% match; -\$114.71, \pv{4.13\!\times\!10^{-48}}). Both underperform \Ag{am_multisignal} (+\$206.13). The linear model's failure confirms the reward landscape is nonlinear.

\subsection{Class-Conditional Label Noise}
Class-conditional noise (crisis$\leftrightarrow$decision, retention$\leftrightarrow$exploration; consideration/opportunity clean) at 10/20/30\% drops \Ag{am_multisignal_learned} match from 98.7\% to 96.3/93.0/89.2\% and profit from +\$205.39 to +\$198.20/+\$186.59/+\$172.27. The 30\% contrast is -\$33.11 (\pv{1.13\!\times\!10^{-34}}). Class-conditional noise is less damaging than uniform noise at the same rate (only 4 of 6 classes corrupted).

\subsection{Convergence Trend Test}
Spearman $\rho$ of profit vs seed index (1..50) and Geweke z (first 20\% vs last 50\%): \Ag{am_multisignal} $\rho = -0.301$ (p = 0.034, marginal), z = 2.04 (borderline); baseline $\rho = 0.189$ (p = 0.188), z = -1.05; \Ag{am_learned} $\rho = -0.176$ (p = 0.221), z = 0.39. The marginal trend reflects higher profits in early seeds, not systematic drift; paired t-tests (per-seed differencing) are robust to it.

\subsection{Perception-Only Dose-Response}
NoisyCAM with flat bidding (bid 1.0, no multiplier) at p$_{correct}$ $\in \{0.5,\ldots,1.0\}$: profit increases monotonically from +\$128.79 (p=0.5, 58.1\% match) to +\$294.27 (p=1.0, 100\% match). Per-seed Spearman $\rho$(profit, p) = 0.998 (min 0.943). Perception-to-profit dose-response is not a bid-policy artifact. p=1.0 (+\$294.27) equals \Ag{ituation_only}, confirming flat bidding is optimal at perfect perception.

\subsection{Crisis Bid Policy Alternatives}
Default crisis response is \Ag{CRISIS_RESPONSE} with 2.0x bid multiplier. Alternatives (50 seeds x 200): default env --- pause (bid 0) +\$225.10, normal (1.0x) +\$215.62, counter (2.0x) +\$206.13; crisis\_heavy --- pause +\$243.79, normal +\$192.87, counter +\$141.93. Pausing consistently beats counter-messaging: the 2.0x multiplier overbids in low-reward crisis situations. This does not invalidate the action mapping (CRISIS$\rightarrow$CRISIS\_RESPONSE) but suggests bid multipliers should be calibrated, not hardcoded.

\subsection{Sensing-Layer Fidelity}
The \Ag{uality_score} signal is a decoy (uniform 0.5--1.0, uncorrelated with the situation-linked \Ag{hannel_quality}); classifiers read \Ag{ontext.channel_quality} directly. With \Ag{ensing_fidelity=True}, all four classifier agents produce identical profits (cam\_multisignal +\$206.13, cam\_multisignal\_learned +\$205.39, cam\_learned +\$155.38, cam\_inferred +\$104.26). The sensing layer is decorative; multi-modal sensing claims should be read as testing classifiers on the three real scalars.

\subsection{Online Reward-Feedback Learning (v1.4)}
\label{sec:online}
The bandit baselines were \textit{offline-trained} (no reward-feedback loop). Two online agents mirror \Ag{am_multisignal} (same 3-signal classifier, same channel rule, same heuristic bid) and differ only in action selection, learned from observed profit within a single 200-step episode:
\begin{itemize}
\item \Ag{nline_eps_q010}: $\varepsilon$-greedy ($\varepsilon{=}0.1$) over (estimated situation $\times$ action) Q-cells, optimistic init Q$_0{=}2.5$, incremental-mean updates.
\item \Ag{nline_ucb}: UCB1 over the same cells (exploration bonus $c{=}0.5$).
\end{itemize}

